        "e": "<strong>Somatic versus germline mutations and evolutionary heritability (Topic 615):</strong> In multicellular organisms, the soma and the germline are developmentally segregated (the Weismann barrier). **Somatic mutations** occur in non-reproductive cells—in this clinical scenario, multipotent hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells residing in the bone marrow exposed to alkylating chemotherapy. When cyclophosphamide induces DNA cross-links and secondary chromosomal translocations in a hematopoietic progenitor, clonal expansion leads to therapy-related acute myeloid leukemia (t-AML). Because hematopoietic cells never contribute genetic material to gametes (spermatozoa or oocytes), somatic mutations are evolutionary dead-ends that perish with the individual and **cannot be inherited** by progeny. Only **germline mutations** arising in gametocytes or primordial germ cells (demonstrated by the spermatogonial experiments in Table 1) are incorporated into gametes and transmitted to offspring.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is false because therapy-related AML arises precisely because mutations were *not* repaired, leading to malignant transformation.<br>&bull; C describes epigenetic silencing of bone marrow mutations during gametogenesis; somatic hematopoietic cells never undergo gametogenesis, so no gametogenic silencing is involved.<br>&bull; D is factually incorrect; leukemia driver mutations (e.g., *TP53* mutations, MLL/KMT2A translocations, monosomy 7) occur in nuclear genomic DNA, not exclusively on mitochondrial DNA."
