        "e": "<strong>Gene dosage and gain-of-function lethality in FGFR3 signaling (Topic 616):</strong> Achondroplasia is caused by a **gain-of-function** mutation in *FGFR3*. FGFR3 normally acts as a physiological brake on chondrocyte proliferation in the epiphyseal plates of growing bones. In heterozygotes (single mutant dose), constitutive tyrosine kinase activity is moderately elevated (345% of WT in Table 1). This slows endochondral ossification sufficiently to produce shortened long bones (rhizomelic micromelia) and characteristic craniofacial features, but bone development is adequate to support life and a normal lifespan. In homozygotes (*FGFR3*<sup>G380R/G380R</sup>), a double gene dosage of constitutively active receptor monomers leads to extreme, unrestricted receptor hyperactivation. This catastrophic hyper-inhibition virtually halts endochondral ossification throughout the skeleton. Most critically, the ribs fail to elongate, resulting in an extremely narrow, constricted thoracic cage. At birth, the rigid, hypoplastic thorax prevents pulmonary expansion, resulting in fatal respiratory insufficiency within hours or days of delivery.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A displays a Loss-of-Function trap; achondroplasia is caused by a constitutive gain-of-function kinase mutation, not an absence of FGFR3 expression.<br>&bull; B describes a lysosomal storage disease pathology (e.g., Gaucher or Tay-Sachs), which does not occur in FGFR3 receptoropathies.<br>&bull; C describes Lyonization (X-inactivation); *FGFR3* is located on autosome 4p16.3, not on the X chromosome, so it never undergoes X-inactivation."
