        "e": "<strong>Paternal age effect and spermatogonial selfish selection (Topic 616):</strong> Over 80% of achondroplasia patients are born to parents of normal stature, representing *de novo* mutations occurring almost exclusively on the paternal chromosome. This paternal bias is driven by fundamental differences in male and female gametogenesis: In human females, the entire pool of primary oocytes is generated during fetal development, undergoing no further mitotic replication after birth. In human males, spermatogonial stem cells (SSCs) in the seminiferous tubules undergo continuous mitotic division throughout post-pubertal life (approximately 23 divisions per year, reaching hundreds of divisions by age 50). Each round of DNA replication introduces a basal probability of replicative polymerase error. Furthermore, the c.1138G&rarr;A mutation in *FGFR3* confers a **selfish selective growth advantage** to mutant spermatogonia: constitutive FGFR3 signaling promotes clonal expansion of mutant SSCs within the testes, leading to an exponential enrichment of mutant sperm as men age (the 'paternal age effect').<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B is biologically false; primary oocytes remain arrested in prophase I from fetal development until ovulation; they do not undergo continuous meiotic crossover in adult life.<br>&bull; C describes an impossible enzymatic activity; DNA glycosylases excise damaged bases to create abasic sites, they do not mutate specific wild-type bases into mutant alleles.<br>&bull; D is a Pathway Misattribution trap; *FGFR3* is biallelically expressed and is not an imprinted gene (unlike *IGF2* or *H19*)."
