    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 5 (Questions 25&ndash;30)</div>\n<p>The transmission and phenotypic expression of eukaryotic genes are modulated by two distinct genetic parameters: penetrance and expressivity. Penetrance is defined quantitatively as the proportion of individuals carrying a specific disease-associated genotype who manifest any recognizable clinical signs of the disorder. Penetrance is an all-or-none population metric; an autosomal dominant allele with 70% penetrance results in clinical symptoms in only 70% of heterozygotes, leaving the remaining 30% non-penetrant despite carrying the mutant allele. In contrast, variable expressivity describes the range or severity of phenotypic manifestations observed among individuals possessing the identical genotype. For example, individuals harboring identical mutations in neurofibromatosis type 1 (<em>NF1</em>) may present with mild cutaneous caf&eacute;-au-lait macules or develop severely disfiguring plexiform neurofibromas and optic pathway gliomas.</p>\n<p>Mendelian segregation assumes that gene pairs assort independently during meiotic gametogenesis, yielding recombinant gamete frequencies of 50%. However, syntenic loci located on the same physical chromosome violate Mendel’s second law if they are situated in close proximity—a phenomenon designated genetic linkage. During prophase I of meiosis, homologous non-sister chromatids undergo reciprocal crossing-over at chiasmata. The recombination frequency (&theta;) between two linked loci is directly proportional to their physical separation:\n$$\\theta = \\frac{\\text{Number of Recombinant Progeny}}{\\text{Total Progeny}} \\times 100\\%$$\nOne percent recombination corresponds to one map unit (m.u.), or one centimorgan (cM). For loci situated on the same chromosome, &theta; approaches a theoretical maximum of 50%, at which point linked genes become indistinguishable from unlinked genes assorting on separate chromosomes.</p>\n<p>Geneticists investigated three linked autosomal loci on chromosome 17 in a mammalian model: <em>N</em> (dominant neurocutaneous dysplasia), <em>B</em> (brachydactyly), and <em>S</em> (spinocerebellar ataxia). A female heterozygous at all three loci in coupling phase (<em>N B S</em> / <em>n b s</em>) was testcrossed to a homozygous recessive male (<em>n b s</em> / <em>n b s</em>). Penetrance of the <em>N</em> allele was measured across two ambient temperature cohorts (22 &deg;C vs 30 &deg;C), and phenotypic severity (expressivity index, 1–10 scale) was scored (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Testcross progeny distribution, recombination classes, and expressivity scores across temperature conditions</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Offspring Phenotype Class</th><th>Gamete From Mother</th><th>Progeny Count (22 &deg;C)</th><th>Progeny Count (30 &deg;C)</th><th>Crossover Classification</th><th>Mean Expressivity of N (1–10)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Class 1: Dysplasia, Brachydactyly, Ataxia</td><td><em>N B S</em></td><td>412</td><td>287 (Plus 123 Non-Penetrant)</td><td>Non-Recombinant (Parental)</td><td>7.8 &plusmn; 0.6</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Class 2: Wild-Type Normal Controls</td><td><em>n b s</em></td><td>408</td><td>410</td><td>Non-Recombinant (Parental)</td><td>N/A (Normal Control)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Class 3: Dysplasia Only</td><td><em>N b s</em></td><td>62</td><td>42 (Plus 18 Non-Penetrant)</td><td>Recombinant (Region I: <em>N</em>&ndash;<em>B</em>)</td><td>7.6 &plusmn; 0.5</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Class 4: Brachydactyly, Ataxia</td><td><em>n B S</em></td><td>58</td><td>60</td><td>Recombinant (Region I: <em>N</em>&ndash;<em>B</em>)</td><td>N/A (Normal Control)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Class 5: Dysplasia, Brachydactyly</td><td><em>N B s</em></td><td>28</td><td>18 (Plus 7 Non-Penetrant)</td><td>Recombinant (Region II: <em>B</em>&ndash;<em>S</em>)</td><td>7.9 &plusmn; 0.7</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Class 6: Ataxia Only</td><td><em>n b S</em></td><td>24</td><td>25</td><td>Recombinant (Region II: <em>B</em>&ndash;<em>S</em>)</td><td>N/A (Normal Control)</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Class 7: Dysplasia, Ataxia</td><td><em>N b S</em></td><td>4</td><td>3 (Plus 2 Non-Penetrant)</td><td>Double Crossover (Regions I &amp; II)</td><td>7.7 &plusmn; 0.6</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Class 8: Brachydactyly Only</td><td><em>n B s</em></td><td>4</td><td>5</td><td>Double Crossover (Regions I &amp; II)</td><td>N/A (Normal Control)</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
