        "e": "<strong>Calculation of expected double crossovers, coefficient of coincidence, and genetic interference (Topic 622):</strong><br>1. **Determine Expected Double Crossovers:** Under the null hypothesis that crossover events in adjacent chromosomal intervals occur independently, the expected probability of a double crossover is the product of the individual single-crossover probabilities:<br>$$P(\\text{Expected DCO}) = \\theta_{\\text{Region I}} \\times \\theta_{\\text{Region II}} = 0.128 \\times 0.060 = 0.00768$$\nFor a sample size of $N = 1000$ progeny, the expected number of double crossover offspring is:<br>$$\\text{Expected DCO} = 0.00768 \\times 1000 = 7.68$$\n2. **Identify Observed Double Crossovers:** The observed double crossovers are Class 7 ($4$) and Class 8 ($4$), totaling **$8$**.<br>3. **Calculate Coefficient of Coincidence ($C$):**<br>$$C = \\frac{\\text{Observed DCO}}{\\text{Expected DCO}} = \\frac{8}{7.68} \\approx 1.04$$\n4. **Interference ($I$):** $I = 1 - C = 1 - 1.04 = -0.04 \\approx 0$. A coefficient of coincidence near $1.0$ indicates that crossing-over in Region I has virtually no inhibitory effect on crossing-over in Region II (negligible genetic interference).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is a math error resulting from dividing observed DCO by only one interval distance.<br>&bull; C describes severe negative interference ($C > 2$), which does not fit the observed data ($8 \\approx 7.68$).<br>&bull; D describes complete interference ($I = 1.0, C = 0$), where no double crossovers would be observed."
