        "e": "<strong>Upper limit of recombination frequency and physical versus genetic linkage (Topic 622):</strong> During meiosis, crossing-over occurs between non-sister chromatids at the four-strand stage. Even if a crossover event occurs between two syntenic loci in 100% of meiotic tetrads, each single crossover involves only two of the four chromatids; the other two non-crossover chromatids remain parental. Consequently, the maximum proportion of recombinant gametes generated by meiotic crossing-over is **50%** ($1/2$). Therefore, when two loci reside on the same chromosome (syntenic) but are physically separated by a large distance (e.g., > 50 map units), multiple crossover events (single, double, triple) occur between them, randomizing alleles such that exactly 50% recombinant and 50% parental gametes are produced. Under standard testcross analysis, a recombination frequency of &theta; = 50% is genetically indistinguishable from two genes located on entirely different, non-homologous chromosomes assorting independently under Mendel’s second law.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes absolute linkage (recombination = 0%), where loci are so physically close that crossovers never occur, yielding 0% recombinants (not 50%).<br>&bull; B is false because 10 cM corresponds to 10% recombination, not 50%; sister chromatid exchanges do not alter allelic recombination.<br>&bull; D describes inversion loops that *suppress* observable recombinant progeny, lowering recombination frequency toward 0% rather than yielding 50%."
