    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 6 (Questions 31&ndash;36)</div>\n<p>Nuclear pre-mRNA splicing is catalyzed by the spliceosome, a multi-megadalton ribonucleoprotein machine composed of five small nuclear ribonucleoproteins (snRNPs: U1, U2, U4, U5, and U6) and over 150 auxiliary non-snRNP splicing factors. Spliceosome assembly proceeds hierarchically along conserved pre-mRNA sequence motifs. Initially, the U1 snRNP recognizes and base-pairs with the 5' splice donor site (consensus containing an invariant GU dinucleotide). Splicing factor 1 (SF1 / BBP) binds the branch-point sequence (BPS), and U2 auxiliary factor (U2AF) binds the polypyrimidine tract and 3' splice acceptor site (invariant AG dinucleotide). Next, U2 snRNP displaces SF1 and base-pairs with the BPS, causing a critical branch-point adenosine nucleotide to bulge outward. The pre-assembled U4/U6.U5 tri-snRNP then joins the complex, forming the pre-catalytic B complex. ATP-dependent RNA helicases trigger extensive conformational rearrangements that displace U1 and U4 snRNPs, allowing U6 snRNA to pair with U2 and juxtapose reactive groups at the catalytic ribozyme center.</p>\n<p>Splicing proceeds through two sequential transesterification reactions: First, the nucleophilic 2'-hydroxyl group of the bulged branch-point adenosine attacks the 5'-phosphate of the guanosine at the 5' splice donor site, releasing the free 5' exon and forming a branched lariat-intermediate containing an unconventional 2'-5' phosphodiester bond. Second, the free 3'-hydroxyl of the upstream exon attacks the 5'-phosphate of the 3' splice acceptor site, ligating the exons and liberating the excised intron lariat.</p>\n<p>Beyond constitutive splicing, alternative splicing allows a single pre-mRNA to yield distinct mature transcripts through exon skipping or mutually exclusive exons, regulated by serine/arginine-rich (SR) splicing activators and heterogeneous nuclear ribonucleoproteins (hnRNPs). Furthermore, mature mRNA sequences can be altered by targeted RNA editing. In human enterocytes, apolipoprotein B mRNA editing catalytic polypeptide 1 (APOBEC-1) cytidine deaminase targets cytidine-6666 of the <em>APOB</em> transcript, deaminating cytidine into uridine. This converts a CAA glutamine codon into an in-frame UAA stop codon, truncating translation to yield ApoB-48 (48% of the full-length hepatic 4,536-residue ApoB-100), directing lipid packaging into chylomicrons rather than VLDL.</p>\n<p>Researchers incubated radiolabeled pre-mRNA minigenes (WT vs branch-point A&rarr;G mutant, or 5' SS GU&rarr;AU mutant) in HeLa nuclear extracts. Splicing intermediates and APOBEC-1 deamination were evaluated (Table 1).</p>\n<table class=\"tbl\">\n  <caption>Table 1 Spliceosomal assembly stages, intermediate yields, and RNA editing efficiency across pre-mRNA constructs</caption>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Construct &amp; Extract Conditions</th><th>Spliceosome Assembly Stage</th><th>Free 5' Exon Formation (%)</th><th>Lariat-Exon 2 Yield (%)</th><th>Mature Ligated mRNA (%)</th><th>C-to-U Editing (%)</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>WT Pre-mRNA + Mock (No ATP)</td><td>Unassembled (Pre-Spliceosome)</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 0.5%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>WT Pre-mRNA + Active Extract</td><td>Fully Catalytic (U2/U6 Center)</td><td>100% (Transient)</td><td>100% (Transient)</td><td>88.5 &plusmn; 3.4%</td><td>&lt; 0.5%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Branch Point (A&rarr;G) Mutant + Active</td><td>Stalled at Pre-Catalytic Stage</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 0.5%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>5' Splice Site (GU&rarr;AU) Mutant + Active</td><td>Blocked at U1 snRNP Binding</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 0.5%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>3' Splice Site (AG&rarr;CG) Mutant + Active</td><td>Stalled Post-1st Transesterification</td><td>94.2 &plusmn; 3.6%</td><td>92.8 &plusmn; 3.5%</td><td>&lt; 1%</td><td>&lt; 0.5%</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>WT Pre-mRNA + Extract + APOBEC-1</td><td>Catalytic Complex + Deaminase</td><td>98.4 &plusmn; 3.2% (Transient)</td><td>97.6 &plusmn; 3.1% (Transient)</td><td>86.2 &plusmn; 3.1%</td><td>89.4 &plusmn; 3.8%</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>",
