        "e": "<strong>Chemical mechanism of the first transesterification reaction in pre-mRNA splicing (Topic 623):</strong> Nuclear pre-mRNA splicing is catalyzed by two sequential transesterification reactions that conserve the total number of phosphodiester bonds without consuming ATP during bond breakage or formation. In the first transesterification step, U2 snRNP base-pairs with the branch-point sequence (BPS), forcing the conserved **branch-point adenosine** to bulge out of the RNA duplex into the active catalytic site formed by U6 and U2 snRNAs. The active site coordinates divalent magnesium ions (Mg<sup>2+</sup>) that activate the **2'-hydroxyl (-OH) group** of this bulged adenosine ribose. The deprotonated 2'-oxygen executes a nucleophilic attack on the 5'-phosphate of the invariant guanosine at the 5' splice donor site (GU). This cleavage liberates the 5' exon (terminating with a free 3'-OH) and covalently connects the 5' end of the intron to the 2'-carbon of the branch-point adenosine, creating a unique **2'-5' phosphodiester linkage** that defines the characteristic intron lariat intermediate.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes the 5' splice site guanosine, which acts as the electrophilic substrate (phosphate attacked), not the initiating nucleophile.<br>&bull; B describes the 5'-phosphate group of adenosine; phosphates are electrophilic centers attacked by nucleophiles, not nucleophiles themselves.<br>&bull; C describes the nucleophile of the *second* transesterification reaction (the free 3'-OH of the 5' exon attacking the 3' splice site)."
