        "e": "<strong>Branch-point adenosine conservation and stereospecificity in splicing (Topic 623):</strong> The branch point requires an invariant **adenosine** residue because the catalytic core of the spliceosome (specifically the RNA cage formed by U2 and U6 snRNAs) exhibits extreme steric and stereochemical specificity. When U2 snRNA pairs with the BPS consensus, the base-pairing pattern forces the branch-point adenosine to bulge un-paired out of the duplex, projecting its 2'-OH directly into the catalytic magnesium coordination shell. When the branch-point adenosine is mutated to guanosine (A to G), the larger bicyclic purine ring and altered hydrogen-bonding potential disrupt base-pairing with U2 snRNA, preventing proper bulging and misaligning the 2'-OH group relative to the catalytic magnesium ions and the 5' splice donor phosphate. Consequently, the first transesterification cannot take place (< 1% free 5' exon and lariat in Table 1). Because exon ligation depends obligatorily on the free 3'-OH generated by the first step, mature ligated mRNA is completely absent.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B is a Pathway / Spatial Misattribution trap; U1 snRNP binds the 5' splice site (GU), which is physically located hundreds to thousands of nucleotides upstream of the branch point.<br>&bull; C is false because RNase H cleaves RNA in RNA:DNA hybrid duplexes, not single-stranded RNA point mutations.<br>&bull; D is false; U2AF binds the downstream polypyrimidine tract and 3' AG dinucleotide, not the branch point, and does not covalently cross-link to guanosine."
