        "e": "<strong>RNA editing of Apolipoprotein B pre-mRNA by APOBEC-1 (Topic 625):</strong> The human *APOB* gene is transcribed from a single genomic locus into a primary pre-mRNA transcript. In hepatocytes (liver), the mRNA is translated without modification into full-length **Apolipoprotein B-100 (ApoB-100)**, a massive 4,536-amino-acid protein that serves as the essential structural scaffold for very-low-density lipoproteins (VLDL) and low-density lipoproteins (LDL) and contains the C-terminal domain required for LDL receptor binding. In enterocytes (small intestine), cells express **APOBEC-1** (apolipoprotein B mRNA editing catalytic polypeptide 1) and its auxiliary factor ACF. APOBEC-1 is a cytidine deaminase that binds an auxiliary mooring sequence in the *APOB* transcript and deaminates the exocyclic amino group of cytidine at nucleotide 6666 into a keto group, converting **cytidine into uridine (C to U)**. This deamination converts a 5'-CAA-3' codon (encoding glutamine at residue 2,153) into an in-frame 5'-UAA-3' **premature termination codon**. Consequently, intestinal translation terminates at residue 2,152, producing **ApoB-48** (exactly 48% of the length of ApoB-100), which is specialized for the assembly and secretion of dietary chylomicrons.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes alternative promoter usage, which is not the mechanism responsible for ApoB isoform diversification.<br>&bull; B describes alternative splicing (exon skipping), but *APOB* undergoes targeted single-base post-transcriptional RNA editing, not exon skipping (Exon 26 is retained in both liver and intestine).<br>&bull; D describes translational ribosomal frameshifting (used by retroviruses like HIV), not mammalian ApoB regulation."
