        "e": "<strong>Spliceosome activation, snRNP rearrangements, and catalytic ribozyme core formation (Topic 623):</strong> The fully assembled pre-catalytic spliceosome (Complex B) contains all five snRNPs (U1, U2, U4, U5, U6) but is catalytically inert because the catalytic sequences are sequestered by inhibitory RNA duplexes. Specifically, U6 snRNA is held in an inactive conformation base-paired extensively with U4 snRNA, while the 5' splice donor site is bound by U1 snRNA. Activation of the spliceosome into the catalytically active **B* complex** requires ATP-dependent RNA helicases (such as Prp28 and Brr2) that catalyze major structural remodelling:<br>1. **Displacement of U1 snRNP** from the 5' splice site, allowing U6 snRNA to take its place and base-pair with the 5' splice donor.<br>2. **Unwinding and release of U4 snRNP**, which liberates U6 snRNA.<br>3. Once freed from U4, U6 snRNA base-pairs with U2 snRNA (which is already bound to the branch point sequence), forming the U2/U6 catalytic RNA center. This RNA-RNA network coordinates the two catalytic magnesium ions, transforming the spliceosome into a protein-directed metallo-ribozyme that executes the first transesterification.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes 3' cleavage and polyadenylation, which occurs downstream at the transcript termination end, not spliceosome catalytic activation.<br>&bull; B describes RNA polymerase II promoter clearance during transcription initiation, which occurs prior to splicing.<br>&bull; C describes translation initiation, which occurs in the cytoplasm on mature mRNAs, completely separate from nuclear pre-mRNA splicing."
