        "e": "<strong>Inosine wobble promiscuity and anticodon decoding versatility (Topic 626):</strong> Francis Crick’s wobble hypothesis demonstrates that the 5' base of the tRNA anticodon (position 34) has greater conformational freedom than the other two positions. Inosine is a purine ribonucleoside containing the nucleobase **hypoxanthine** (formed by hydrolytic deamination of adenine by TadA). Hypoxanthine possesses a 6-keto oxygen and a protonated <em>N</em><sup>1</sup> nitrogen, but lacks the exocyclic 2-amino group found in guanine. Due to this unique hydrogen-bonding donor/acceptor profile, inosine at the wobble position can form stable, two-hydrogen-bond pairs with three different RNA bases at the 3' position of the mRNA codon:<br>&bull; $I : U$ (wobble pair)<br>&bull; $I : C$ (wobble pair)<br>&bull; $I : A$ (wobble pair)<br>Inosine cannot pair with guanosine due to severe steric clash and repulsive electrostatic interactions between the keto groups. Consequently, a single tRNA harboring inosine at position 34 (such as $\text{tRNA}^{\text{IAU}}$ in Table 1) can efficiently decode three synonymous codons: $5'\text{-AUU-}3'$, $5'\text{-AUC-}3'$, and $5'\text{-AUA-}3'$ (all achieving > 95% decoding in Table 1).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A is false because inosine cannot pair with guanosine due to electrostatic repulsion between their C-6 carbonyl oxygens.<br>&bull; C describes pairing exclusively with purines, but inosine pairs with pyrimidines (U, C) as well as the purine A.<br>&bull; D describes exclusive pairing with uridine, which ignores inosine's well-established promiscuity with C and A."
