        "a": 2,
        "e": "<strong>Hydrolytic editing site architecture and uncoupling of synthetic and editing functions (Topic 627):</strong> Aminoacyl-tRNA synthetases utilize two physically distinct active sites separated by over 30 Å: the **synthetic activation site** (first sieve) and the **hydrolytic editing site** (second sieve). Because valine is smaller than isoleucine by a single methylene group ($-\text{CH}_2-$), it is misactivated in the synthetic pocket at a low frequency (~1 in 200). In wild-type IleRS, when valine is transferred to the tRNA to generate mischarged $\text{Val-tRNA}^{\text{Ile}}$, the flexible 3' acceptor CCA arm translocates into the editing site. Conserved residues in the editing pocket (including Thr242) orient a catalytic water molecule that rapidly hydrolyzes the mischarged ester bond (post-transfer editing), keeping the net error rate below 0.01% (Table 1). The Thr242Pro mutation selectively disrupts the architecture of the editing pocket without disturbing the distant synthetic activation site. As a result, isoleucine activation and charging proceed with normal catalytic efficiency ($4.6 \times 10^4\text{ s}^{-1}\text{mM}^{-1}$), but misactivated valyl intermediates can no longer be hydrolyzed. Consequently, mischarged $\text{Val-tRNA}^{\text{Ile}}$ escapes into the translation pool, driving the error rate up to 14.8%.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes alteration of the synthetic activation site, but Table 1 shows that valine catalytic efficiency ($k_{cat}/K_M \u0007pprox 1.1 \times 10^2$) and isoleucine efficiency remain unchanged between WT and T242P.<br>&bull; B is false because tRNA binding is intact, as evidenced by normal isoleucine charging and decoding (> 95%).<br>&bull; D is biologically absurd; the editing pocket is a hydrolytic esterase, not an ATP-dependent aminoacyl-AMP synthase."
