        "a": 3,
        "e": "<strong>Wobble base-pairing rules for guanosine at anticodon position 34 (Topic 626):</strong> According to Crick's wobble rules, the base at the $5'$ position (position 34) of the tRNA anticodon pairs with the base at the $3'$ position of the mRNA codon according to strict conformational geometries:<br>&bull; When **$G$** is at anticodon position 34, it can form standard Watson-Crick base pairs with **$C$** ($G:C$, three hydrogen bonds) and non-canonical wobble pairs with **$U$** ($G:U$, two hydrogen bonds).<br>&bull; However, guanosine ($G$) cannot form stable base pairs with adenosine ($A$) or another guanosine ($G$) because purine-purine pairings create steric bulk that distorts the phosphodiester backbone, failing the geometric inspection of the 16S/18S ribosomal decoding center.<br>Therefore, the $\text{tRNA}^{\text{GAU}}$ construct can efficiently decode the isoleucine codons $5'\text{-AUU-}3'$ (via $G:U$ wobble, 96.5%) and $5'\text{-AUC-}3'$ (via $G:C$ Watson-Crick, 98.2%), but completely fails to decode $5'\text{-AUA-}3'$ (only 4.1% in Table 1). Decoding $5'\text{-AUA-}3'$ requires either an inosine-modified tRNA ($\text{tRNA}^{\text{IAU}}$) or a specific tRNA harboring modified uridine at position 34.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes G methylation, but G34 does participate in wobble pairing with U (96.5% decoding), disproving the claim that all non-Watson-Crick interactions are prevented.<br>&bull; B is false because the ribosome does not hydrolyze tRNAs based on G34 presence.<br>&bull; C describes irreversible binding via three hydrogen bonds; $G$ and $A$ are both purines and do not form three Watson-Crick hydrogen bonds."
