        "e": "<strong>Steric exclusion and the double-sieve proofreading mechanism (Topic 627):</strong> The double-sieve model explains how an enzyme achieves near-perfect fidelity when discriminating between two chemically similar substrates where one is slightly smaller than the other (isoleucine vs valine):<br>1. **First Sieve (Coarse Sieve / Synthetic Pocket):** The synthetic activation pocket is sized to bind the correct cognate substrate (**isoleucine**). Amino acids that are *larger* than isoleucine (such as leucine, phenylalanine, or tryptophan) are sterically excluded from the synthetic site by van der Waals clash and cannot be activated.<br>2. **Second Sieve (Fine Sieve / Editing Pocket):** Amino acids that are *smaller* than isoleucine (such as **valine**, which lacks the extra $-\text{CH}_2-$ group on its hydrocarbon tail) can slip into the synthetic pocket and undergo activation. To prevent these errors, the synthetase translocates the aminoacyl moiety into the hydrolytic editing pocket. Crucially, the editing pocket is **sterically narrower** than the synthetic site: it is precisely dimensioned to accommodate valine and smaller side chains, but is **too small to fit the bulkier side chain of isoleucine**. Because isoleucine cannot enter the editing pocket, correctly charged $\text{Ile-tRNA}^{\text{Ile}}$ is protected from futile hydrolysis, while mischarged $\text{Val-tRNA}^{\text{Ile}}$ is rapidly destroyed.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B describes electrostatic salt bridges, but isoleucine and valine are both nonpolar aliphatic amino acids.<br>&bull; C describes charge repulsion, but both isoleucine and valine side chains are uncharged hydrocarbons.<br>&bull; D is a chemical error; amino acids are attached to tRNA via an **ester bond** at the 2'- or 3'-hydroxyl of the terminal adenosine ribose, not an amide bond."
