        "e": "<strong>Degeneracy of the genetic code and silent (synonymous) mutations (Topic 626):</strong> The genetic code is described as **degenerate** (or redundant) because multiple distinct trinucleotide codons encode the same amino acid (for example, leucine and arginine each have 6 codons, and isoleucine has 3). This degeneracy is clustered predominantly at the third (3') position of the codon, directly matching the flexibility of wobble base-pairing. From an evolutionary perspective, this provides a vital protective buffer against spontaneous mutations: Spontaneous transition or transversion mutations that occur at the third base of a coding triplet frequently convert one codon into another codon that translates to the **exact same amino acid**—a **silent (synonymous) mutation**. For example, mutating $5'\text{-AUU-}3'$ to $5'\text{-AUC-}3'$ or $5'\text{-AUA-}3'$ still yields isoleucine. Consequently, the primary amino acid sequence, folding thermodynamics, and enzymatic function of the resulting protein remain completely unaffected, buffering the organism against deleterious phenotypic consequences.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A confuses translation wobble with transcription proofreading; RNA polymerases do not use wobble rules to proofread pre-mRNA.<br>&bull; B describes missense mutations and displays complete Directional Inversion; degeneracy *minimizes* amino acid changes rather than ensuring them.<br>&bull; D describes DNA mismatch repair, which operates on double-stranded DNA replication errors, not messenger RNA translation."
