        "a": 3,
        "e": "<strong>Thermodynamics and enzymatic mechanism of tRNA aminoacylation (Topic 627):</strong> Attaching an amino acid to tRNA requires the formation of a high-energy ester linkage between the amino acid carboxylate ($-\text{COO}^-$) and the 2'- or 3'-hydroxyl group of the terminal adenosine of the tRNA CCA-3' stem. This is an endergonic condensation reaction driven by ATP cleavage in a two-step mechanism:<br>1. **Activation (Adenylation):** The carboxylate oxygen of the amino acid mounts a nucleophilic attack on the &alpha;-phosphate of ATP, displacing inorganic pyrophosphate ($\text{PP}_i$) and forming an enzyme-bound **aminoacyl-AMP** (aminoacyl-adenylate) intermediate containing a mixed anhydride bond.<br>2. **Transfer:** The 2'- or 3'-OH of the terminal adenosine attacks the carbonyl carbon of the aminoacyl-AMP, transferring the amino acid onto the tRNA and releasing AMP.<br>3. **Thermodynamic Coupling:** The inorganic pyrophosphate ($\text{PP}_i$) released in step 1 is immediately hydrolyzed into two molecules of inorganic orthophosphate ($2\text{ P}_i$) by ubiquitous inorganic pyrophosphatase ($\\Delta G^\\circ < -19\text{ kJ/mol}$). This irreversible hydrolysis shifts the overall equilibrium strongly forward, consuming the equivalent of **two high-energy phosphoanhydride bonds** ($ATP \rightarrow AMP + 2\text{ P}_i$).<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes GTP hydrolysis, but aminoacylation is strictly ATP-dependent (GTP is used during elongation factors EF-Tu and EF-G, not aaRS activation).<br>&bull; B describes tRNA phosphorylation and ATP cleavage to ADP; aaRS cleaves ATP to AMP and pyrophosphate, and attaches the amino acid directly rather than phosphorylating the tRNA.<br>&bull; C describes thioester formation with a cysteine (used in ubiquitin activation by E1 enzymes, not aaRS)."
