        "e": "<strong>Peptidyl transferase ribozyme catalysis in translation elongation (Topic 628):</strong> The peptidyl transferase center (PTC) is located within the large ribosomal subunit (50S in prokaryotes, 60S in eukaryotes). Structural and biochemical studies have revealed that the PTC contains no protein side chains within 18 Å of the catalytic site; instead, peptidyl transferase activity is catalyzed entirely by conserved ribonucleotides of the large ribosomal RNA (**28S rRNA** in eukaryotes, **23S rRNA** in prokaryotes). The ribosome is therefore a **ribozyme** (an RNA enzyme). The rRNA active site provides an entropic catalytic trap: it positions the &alpha;-amino group of the incoming aminoacyl-tRNA residing in the ribosomal **A (aminoacyl) site** in precise stereochemical alignment to mount a nucleophilic attack on the ester carbonyl carbon of the peptidyl-tRNA residing in the **P (peptidyl) site**. Hydrogen bonds with rRNA adenine residues (e.g., A2451 in *E. coli*) and the 2'-OH of the P-site tRNA terminal ribose stabilize the tetrahedral oxyanion transition state, transferring the growing polypeptide chain onto the A-site tRNA.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; B describes classic protein enzyme catalytic triad mechanisms (like serine proteases with histidine/aspartate), but the ribosome utilizes RNA catalysis without catalytic protein side chains.<br>&bull; C describes EF-Tu/eEF1A, which hydrolyzes GTP to proofread codon-anticodon recognition and release the aminoacyl-tRNA into the A site, not to catalyze peptide bond formation.<br>&bull; D places peptidyl transferase in the small subunit (18S rRNA) and E site; the small subunit mediates mRNA decoding, while peptide bond synthesis occurs in the large subunit (28S rRNA) at the A/P interface."
