        "e": "<strong>eIF2alpha phosphorylation, eIF2B sequestration, and translational control (Topic 629):</strong> Eukaryotic translation initiation requires the formation of the **ternary complex**, consisting of **eIF2 &bull; GTP &bull; Met-tRNA<sub>i</sub><sup>Met</sup>**, which binds the 40S subunit to form the 43S pre-initiation complex. During start codon recognition, eIF2 hydrolyzes its bound GTP to GDP and dissociates as an inactive **eIF2 &bull; GDP** binary complex. To participate in subsequent rounds of initiation, eIF2 must be recharged by its guanine nucleotide exchange factor (GEF), **eIF2B**, which exchanges GDP for fresh GTP. Under endoplasmic reticulum stress (unfolded protein response), viral infection, amino acid deprivation, or heme deficiency, stress-sensing kinases (PERK, PKR, GCN2, HRI) phosphorylate eIF2 on **Ser51 of the &alpha; subunit**. Phosphorylated eIF2&alpha; exhibits a 100-fold increase in binding affinity for eIF2B, turning eIF2 from a transient substrate into a tight, competitive inhibitor. Because eIF2B is present in much lower molar abundance than eIF2 in cells, a modest fraction of phosphorylated eIF2&alpha; completely titrates out all cellular eIF2B. Without eIF2B exchange activity, eIF2 remains locked in the inactive GDP-bound state, depleting ternary complexes and shutting down global protein synthesis to conserve cellular resources.<br><br><em>Distractor Traps:</em><br>&bull; A describes direct steric blocking of ribosomal subunits, but phosphorylated eIF2 acts biochemically by sequestering the eIF2B GEF in the cytoplasm.<br>&bull; B describes mRNA deadenylation (CCR4-NOT complex), which is not the direct consequence of eIF2&alpha; phosphorylation.<br>&bull; D describes decapping (Dcp1/2), whereas eIF2 regulates initiator tRNA delivery, while cap recognition is mediated by the separate eIF4F complex (eIF4E)."
